---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/indas/indas32.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.summary}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope & Applicability" },
        { id: "s3", label: "The Substance-Over-Form Classification Test" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Compound Financial Instruments" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Puttable Instruments Exception" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Treasury Shares" },
        { id: "s7", label: "CCPS & Redeemable Preference Shares" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Offsetting Financial Assets and Liabilities" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Critical Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Ind AS 32 vs IAS 32 — Full Comparison" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Quick-Reference Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <!-- ============ SECTION 1 — CONCEPTUAL FRAMEWORK LINK ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci"><div class="ctitle ci">Identical substance-over-form philosophy to IAS 32</div>
        <div class="principle">Ind AS 32 applies the IDENTICAL principle as IAS 32 — the classification of a financial instrument as a LIABILITY or EQUITY is based on its SUBSTANCE, not merely its legal form, turning on whether the issuer has a CONTRACTUAL OBLIGATION to deliver cash/another financial asset.</div>
        <div class="elabel">HOW IND AS 32 MAPS TO THE FRAMEWORK</div>
        <div class="check-list"><span><strong>Faithful representation</strong> — an instrument obligating the issuer to transfer economic resources is a liability REGARDLESS of its legal label (e.g. "preference share") — identical to IAS 32</span><span><strong>Widespread Indian application</strong> — Compulsorily Convertible Preference Shares (CCPS) are the DOMINANT funding instrument in Indian startup/venture capital transactions, and redeemable preference shares are common in family business succession/estate planning structures, making Ind AS 32's classification test GENUINELY significant in Indian practice</span></div>
        <div class="note">Ind AS 32 introduced NO substantive change to the classification model relative to IAS 32 — its Indian relevance is most pronounced for the SCALE and FREQUENCY of CCPS/redeemable preference share structures.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cs"><div class="ctitle cs">The core classification test — identical to IAS 32</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><div class="f-eq">Contractual obligation to deliver cash/another financial asset = LIABILITY (not equity), regardless of legal form</div></div>
        <div class="callout info callout--flush">IDENTICAL test to IAS 32.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 2 — SCOPE & DEFINITIONS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope &amp; Applicability</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw"><div class="ctitle cw">What Ind AS 32 covers</div><div class="principle">Ind AS 32 establishes principles for presenting financial instruments as liabilities or equity, and for offsetting financial assets/liabilities — IDENTICAL scope to IAS 32, working alongside Ind AS 109's recognition/measurement rules and Ind AS 107's disclosure requirements.</div></div>
      <div class="card cd"><div class="ctitle cd">Key definitions you must know</div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Financial liability</div><div class="glossary-def">IDENTICAL definition to IAS 32 — a contractual obligation to deliver cash/another financial asset, or a potentially unfavourable exchange of financial instruments.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">CCPS</div><div class="glossary-def">Compulsorily Convertible Preference Shares — a preference share MANDATORILY converting into equity shares, the dominant Indian venture funding instrument.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 3 — SUBSTANCE-OVER-FORM TEST (worked example) ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>The Substance-Over-Form Classification Test (with worked example)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Identical to IAS 32</div>
      <div class="worked worked--flush"><div class="wk-title">Example 1: Redeemable preference shares — liability, not equity</div><div class="wk-body">An Indian family-owned company issues REDEEMABLE preference shares to a family member, with a CONTRACTUAL obligation to redeem (repay) the shares at a fixed date for a fixed cash amount.</div><div class="wk-answer">Despite being legally labelled "preference SHARES" (typically associated with equity), this instrument is classified as a FINANCIAL LIABILITY, because the issuer has a contractual obligation to deliver CASH — an IDENTICAL substance-over-form analysis to how IAS 32 would classify the same instrument globally, illustrating why LEGAL form (share capital under company law) does NOT determine Ind AS 32 classification.</div></div>
    </div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 4 — COMPOUND FINANCIAL INSTRUMENTS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Compound Financial Instruments</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Identical split-accounting approach to IAS 32</div><div class="text-supporting">Instruments with BOTH a liability and equity component (e.g. convertible bonds) are SPLIT into their component parts at issuance — IDENTICAL to IAS 32, with the liability component measured first and the RESIDUAL allocated to equity.</div></div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 5 — PUTTABLE INSTRUMENTS EXCEPTION ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Puttable Instruments Exception</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Identical narrow exception to IAS 32</div><div class="text-supporting">Certain puttable instruments meeting SPECIFIC criteria (e.g. entitling the holder to a pro-rata share of net assets on liquidation) may be classified as EQUITY despite the put feature — IDENTICAL narrow exception to IAS 32, relevant for certain Indian partnership/LLP-adjacent structures.</div></div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 6 — TREASURY SHARES ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Treasury Shares</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Identical to IAS 32</div><div class="text-supporting">An entity's own equity instruments reacquired (treasury shares) are DEDUCTED from equity, with NO gain/loss recognised in P&amp;L on purchase, sale, issue, or cancellation — IDENTICAL to IAS 32, relevant for Indian companies conducting share buybacks under the Companies Act, 2013.</div></div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 7 — CCPS & REDEEMABLE PREFERENCE SHARES (worked example) ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>CCPS &amp; Redeemable Preference Shares — Indian Application Context (with worked example)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A genuinely important, practically prevalent Indian application area</div>
      <div class="principle">CCPS are the DOMINANT financial instrument in Indian venture capital/private equity funding rounds, typically carrying preferential rights (liquidation preference, anti-dilution) alongside MANDATORY conversion to equity shares (often on a specified date or triggering event, such as an IPO). Because the CONVERSION is mandatory into a FIXED (or determinable) number of equity shares, CCPS are typically classified as EQUITY under Ind AS 32's fixed-for-fixed test — but any ATTACHED features (e.g. a redemption right if conversion conditions are not met, or a variable conversion ratio) require CAREFUL analysis, potentially triggering liability or compound instrument classification.</div>
      <div class="worked worked--flush"><div class="wk-title">Example 2: CCPS with a fixed conversion ratio</div><div class="wk-body">An Indian startup issues CCPS to a venture capital investor, with MANDATORY conversion into a FIXED number of equity shares upon a qualified IPO or after 20 years, whichever is earlier, with NO redemption option for cash.</div><div class="wk-answer">Since the CCPS convert into a FIXED number of equity shares with NO cash redemption obligation, they are classified as EQUITY under Ind AS 32's fixed-for-fixed test — an IDENTICAL classification outcome to how IAS 32 would treat a comparable mandatorily convertible instrument globally.</div></div>
    </div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 8 — OFFSETTING ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Offsetting Financial Assets and Liabilities</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Identical to IAS 32</div><div class="text-supporting">Offsetting is permitted ONLY when a CURRENTLY ENFORCEABLE legal right to set off exists AND the entity intends to settle net (or simultaneously) — IDENTICAL criteria to IAS 32.</div></div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 9 — DISCLOSURE ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Identical disclosure requirements to IAS 32 (largely via Ind AS 107)</div><div class="check-list"><span>Classification rationale for compound/borderline instruments</span><span>Offsetting arrangements and net/gross presentation</span><span>Terms and conditions of CCPS/redeemable preference shares affecting classification</span></div></div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 10 — JUDGMENTS & ESTIMATES ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where management judgement matters most</div>
      <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Liability vs equity classification for CCPS/redeemable preference shares with complex embedded features</span><span>Split-accounting allocation for compound instruments</span></div></div>
      <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Fair value of the liability component in compound instrument split-accounting</span></div></div></div>
      <div class="callout warn callout--warn-flush">CCPS term sheets in Indian venture deals often contain COMPLEX anti-dilution, liquidation preference, and conditional redemption clauses requiring careful Ind AS 32 analysis — a genuinely common area of practical complexity.</div>
    </div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 11 — IND AS 32 vs IAS 32 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Ind AS 32 vs IAS 32 — Full Comparison</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comprehensive comparison table</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th cols-12-1-1"><span>Feature</span><span>IAS 32</span><span>Ind AS 32</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Liability/equity classification, compound instruments, offsetting</span><span>Standard model</span><span>IDENTICAL — no substantive divergence</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-12-1-1"><span>Practical application context</span><span>Varies by jurisdiction/market</span><span>Especially prominent given India's CCPS-dominant venture funding market and family business redeemable preference share structures</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">Ind AS 32 is essentially IDENTICAL to IAS 32 in substance — its distinctive Indian relevance lies in the SCALE and FREQUENCY of CCPS/redeemable preference share applications, not any accounting divergence.</div>
    </div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 12 — STATEMENT MAPPING ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Comprehensive event-to-statement mapping table</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event / transaction</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Liability-classified instrument</span><span><span class="badge bs">Balance Sheet</span></span><span>Presented as a financial liability</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Equity-classified instrument (e.g. fixed-for-fixed CCPS)</span><span><span class="badge be">Equity</span></span><span>Presented within equity, identical to IAS 32</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Compound instrument split</span><span><span class="badge bs">Balance Sheet</span> <span class="badge be">Equity</span></span><span>Liability and equity components presented separately</span></div>
      </div>
    </div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 13 — RELATED STANDARDS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
        <div class="td-table"><div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>IAS 32</span><span>The global counterpart standard, near-identically converged</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>Ind AS 109</span><span>Governs recognition and measurement of the financial instruments Ind AS 32 classifies</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>Ind AS 107</span><span>Supplies the detailed disclosure requirements for classified financial instruments</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn"><div class="ctitle cn">Quick-reference glossary</div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Fixed-for-fixed test</div><div class="glossary-def">An instrument settled by delivering a FIXED number of equity shares for a FIXED amount of cash qualifies as an equity instrument — identical to IAS 32.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Liquidation preference</div><div class="glossary-def">A common CCPS term entitling the investor to priority proceeds on liquidation/exit, relevant to classification analysis.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 14 — GLOSSARY ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <Glossary entries={[
      { term: "Financial liability", definition: "IDENTICAL definition to IAS 32 — a contractual obligation to deliver cash/another financial asset, or a potentially unfavourable exchange of financial instruments." },
      { term: "CCPS", definition: "Compulsorily Convertible Preference Shares — a preference share MANDATORILY converting into equity shares, the dominant Indian venture funding instrument." },
      { term: "Fixed-for-fixed test", definition: "An instrument settled by delivering a FIXED number of equity shares for a FIXED amount of cash qualifies as an equity instrument — identical to IAS 32." },
      { term: "Liquidation preference", definition: "A common CCPS term entitling the investor to priority proceeds on liquidation/exit, relevant to classification analysis." },
    ]} />

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
